  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-807",
              "name": "Rate-limit bypass in public contact endpoint via spoofed forwarding headers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Rate-limit bypass in public contact endpoint via spoofed forwarding headers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The public **`/api/contact`** endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied forwarding headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to evade the intended spam throttle by changing **`X-Forwarded-For`** or **`X-Real-IP`** values. This keeps attacker-controlled requests flowing into the service-role-backed contact ingestion path despite the presence of a limiter."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The public **`/api/contact`** endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied forwarding headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to evade the intended spam throttle by changing **`X-Forwarded-For`** or **`X-Real-IP`** values. This keeps attacker-controlled requests flowing into the service-role-backed contact ingestion path despite the presence of a limiter.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can bypass the contact form's anti-automation control and continue submitting arbitrary lead data into the CRM ingestion flow by rotating spoofed IP headers. This permits unauthorized manipulation of CRM records at scale and defeats the endpoint's intended abuse-prevention boundary, even though the downstream insert path is protected only by server-side service-role credentials.\n\n1. Derive the rate-limit key from a server-trusted client address provided by the runtime or hosting platform, not from raw request headers.\n2. For Astro server routes, prefer the framework-provided client address value and fall back to a safe sentinel when it is unavailable.\n3. Treat **`X-Forwarded-For`** and **`X-Real-IP`** as untrusted unless they have been sanitized by a specifically trusted reverse proxy under application control.\n4. Apply the same rule consistently anywhere the shared IP helper is used for security decisions, including other throttled endpoints.",
                "markdown": "The public **`/api/contact`** endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied forwarding headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to evade the intended spam throttle by changing **`X-Forwarded-For`** or **`X-Real-IP`** values. This keeps attacker-controlled requests flowing into the service-role-backed contact ingestion path despite the presence of a limiter.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can bypass the contact form's anti-automation control and continue submitting arbitrary lead data into the CRM ingestion flow by rotating spoofed IP headers. This permits unauthorized manipulation of CRM records at scale and defeats the endpoint's intended abuse-prevention boundary, even though the downstream insert path is protected only by server-side service-role credentials.\n\n1. Derive the rate-limit key from a server-trusted client address provided by the runtime or hosting platform, not from raw request headers.\n2. For Astro server routes, prefer the framework-provided client address value and fall back to a safe sentinel when it is unavailable.\n3. Treat **`X-Forwarded-For`** and **`X-Real-IP`** as untrusted unless they have been sanitized by a specifically trusted reverse proxy under application control.\n4. Apply the same rule consistently anywhere the shared IP helper is used for security decisions, including other throttled endpoints."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-807",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-307",
              "name": "Authentication Rate-Limit Bypass in Dashboard Login via Spoofed Proxy Headers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authentication Rate-Limit Bypass in Dashboard Login via Spoofed Proxy Headers"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The dashboard login endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied proxy headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to rotate `x-real-ip` values and bypass the login throttle. This removes the intended online guessing protection on the administrative login."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The dashboard login endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied proxy headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to rotate `x-real-ip` values and bypass the login throttle. This removes the intended online guessing protection on the administrative login.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can make unlimited password attempts against `POST /dashboard/login` by changing spoofed proxy IP headers between requests. This bypasses the only demonstrated brute-force protection on the administrative login and materially weakens the protection of the dashboard session boundary.\n\n1. Derive the dashboard login rate-limit key from a server-controlled client address, such as Astro's `clientAddress`, instead of directly trusting `x-real-ip` or `x-forwarded-for` from the request.\n2. Keep proxy-header parsing behind an explicit trusted-proxy boundary if those headers must be used elsewhere.\n3. Re-test the login flow to ensure that repeated failed attempts from the same client are throttled even when proxy headers are rotated.\n4. Review other uses of the shared rate-limit helper and update them to use the same server-controlled client identity source where appropriate.",
                "markdown": "The dashboard login endpoint derives its rate-limit key from client-supplied proxy headers, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to rotate `x-real-ip` values and bypass the login throttle. This removes the intended online guessing protection on the administrative login.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can make unlimited password attempts against `POST /dashboard/login` by changing spoofed proxy IP headers between requests. This bypasses the only demonstrated brute-force protection on the administrative login and materially weakens the protection of the dashboard session boundary.\n\n1. Derive the dashboard login rate-limit key from a server-controlled client address, such as Astro's `clientAddress`, instead of directly trusting `x-real-ip` or `x-forwarded-for` from the request.\n2. Keep proxy-header parsing behind an explicit trusted-proxy boundary if those headers must be used elsewhere.\n3. Re-test the login flow to ensure that repeated failed attempts from the same client are throttled even when proxy headers are rotated.\n4. Review other uses of the shared rate-limit helper and update them to use the same server-controlled client identity source where appropriate."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-307",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/307.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-613",
              "name": "Logout Does Not Invalidate Existing Dashboard Sessions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Logout Does Not Invalidate Existing Dashboard Sessions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The dashboard logout flow deletes the browser cookie but does not invalidate the underlying administrator session. A previously copied `crm_session` token remains valid after logout and continues to grant access to protected CRM pages until the cookie expires."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The dashboard logout flow deletes the browser cookie but does not invalidate the underlying administrator session. A previously copied `crm_session` token remains valid after logout and continues to grant access to protected CRM pages until the cookie expires.\n\nAn attacker who has obtained a valid `crm_session` cookie can continue accessing protected dashboard content after the legitimate user logs out. The demonstrated impact is continued unauthorized read access to CRM lead data exposed through `/dashboard/leads` and related protected dashboard routes until the session naturally expires.\n\n1. Replace the stateless dashboard cookie with a session model that has server-side revocation state.\n2. Register each issued session in a server-side store, keyed by a session identifier carried in the signed cookie.\n3. On logout, revoke that server-side record before deleting the browser cookie.\n4. In middleware, require both a valid signature and an active, non-revoked server-side session record before granting access to `/dashboard/*`.\n5. Fail closed when the session registry cannot be queried, and periodically purge expired session records.",
                "markdown": "The dashboard logout flow deletes the browser cookie but does not invalidate the underlying administrator session. A previously copied `crm_session` token remains valid after logout and continues to grant access to protected CRM pages until the cookie expires.\n\nAn attacker who has obtained a valid `crm_session` cookie can continue accessing protected dashboard content after the legitimate user logs out. The demonstrated impact is continued unauthorized read access to CRM lead data exposed through `/dashboard/leads` and related protected dashboard routes until the session naturally expires.\n\n1. Replace the stateless dashboard cookie with a session model that has server-side revocation state.\n2. Register each issued session in a server-side store, keyed by a session identifier carried in the signed cookie.\n3. On logout, revoke that server-side record before deleting the browser cookie.\n4. In middleware, require both a valid signature and an active, non-revoked server-side session record before granting access to `/dashboard/*`.\n5. Fail closed when the session registry cannot be queried, and periodically purge expired session records."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-613",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/613.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
