          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "db06ccdb59285c5bca48392276e237633a9e4f450dafd9dbe0c9a4e51531dfff",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-26 13:44:54 UTC",
              "target": "OrPiRa portfolio application",
              "endpoint": "/api/contact",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-807",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can bypass the contact form's anti-automation control and continue submitting arbitrary lead data into the CRM ingestion flow by rotating spoofed IP headers. This permits unauthorized manipulation of CRM records at scale and defeats the endpoint's intended abuse-prevention boundary, even though the downstream insert path is protected only by server-side service-role credentials.",
              "technical_analysis": "The contact ingestion route calls the limiter with **`getClientIp(request)`** before inserting into Supabase:\n\n```ts title=src/pages/api/contact.ts startLineNumber=95 endLineNumber=95\n\t\tif (isRateLimited(getClientIp(request))) {\n```\n\nThe shared helper used for this decision trusts attacker-controlled headers:\n\n```ts title=src/lib/rate-limit.ts startLineNumber=34 endLineNumber=53\nexport const getClientIp = (request: Request) => {\n\tconst realIp = request.headers.get(\"x-real-ip\")?.trim();\n\tif (realIp) {\n\t\treturn realIp;\n\t}\n\n\tconst forwarded = request.headers.get(\"x-forwarded-for\");\n\tif (forwarded) {\n\t\tconst candidates = forwarded\n\t\t\t.split(\",\")\n\t\t\t.map((value) => value.trim())\n\t\t\t.filter(Boolean);\n\n\t\tif (candidates.length > 0) {\n\t\t\treturn candidates[candidates.length - 1];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn \"unknown\";\n};\n```\n\nBecause the endpoint accepts these values directly from the request, the limiter key is attacker-chosen rather than server-derived. The result is a security decision based on untrusted input: once one spoofed address reaches the threshold, switching to a different header value immediately restores access to the pre-limit path. The route then continues into the service-role-backed **`supabase.from(\"contacts\").insert(...)`** operation.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Derive the rate-limit key from a server-trusted client address provided by the runtime or hosting platform, not from raw request headers.\n2. For Astro server routes, prefer the framework-provided client address value and fall back to a safe sentinel when it is unavailable.\n3. Treat **`X-Forwarded-For`** and **`X-Real-IP`** as untrusted unless they have been sanitized by a specifically trusted reverse proxy under application control.\n4. Apply the same rule consistently anywhere the shared IP helper is used for security decisions, including other throttled endpoints.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send repeated unauthenticated **`POST`** requests to **`/api/contact`** with a valid JSON body and a fixed spoofed **`X-Forwarded-For`** value.\n2. Observe that the first eight requests are processed normally and the ninth request returns **`429`**.\n3. Resend the same request immediately, changing only the spoofed forwarding header to a different IP value.\n4. Observe that the response returns to a non-**`429`** status, proving the limiter key is derived from attacker-controlled headers rather than a trusted server-side client address.\n5. Confirm that the non-**`429`** requests still reach the contact ingestion path, evidenced by downstream processing responses instead of rate-limit rejection.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
