          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "289f6a910aafdffa6a725aab9ba77d41f58a8455578346e4b0facf8e94a8a249",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-26 13:49:38 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/OrPiRa-portfolio",
              "endpoint": "/dashboard/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-307",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can make unlimited password attempts against `POST /dashboard/login` by changing spoofed proxy IP headers between requests. This bypasses the only demonstrated brute-force protection on the administrative login and materially weakens the protection of the dashboard session boundary.",
              "technical_analysis": "The dashboard login route enforces throttling before password verification:\n\n```ts title=src/pages/dashboard/login.ts startLineNumber=30 endLineNumber=43\nexport const POST: APIRoute = async ({\n  request,\n  cookies,\n  redirect\n}) => {\n\n  const form =\n    await request.formData();\n\n  const password =\n    form.get(\"password\");\n\n  if (isRateLimited(getClientIp(request))) {\n    return redirect(\"/dashboard?error=2\", 303);\n  }\n```\n\nThe rate-limit key comes from `getClientIp(request)`, which trusts `x-real-ip` first and then `x-forwarded-for`:\n\n```ts title=src/lib/rate-limit.ts startLineNumber=34 endLineNumber=52\nexport const getClientIp = (request: Request) => {\n\tconst realIp = request.headers.get(\"x-real-ip\")?.trim();\n\tif (realIp) {\n\t\treturn realIp;\n\t}\n\n\tconst forwarded = request.headers.get(\"x-forwarded-for\");\n\tif (forwarded) {\n\t\tconst candidates = forwarded\n\t\t\t.split(\",\")\n\t\t\t.map((value) => value.trim())\n\t\t\t.filter(Boolean);\n\n\t\tif (candidates.length > 0) {\n\t\t\treturn candidates[candidates.length - 1];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn \"unknown\";\n};\n```\n\nBecause these headers are accepted directly from the request, the client controls the limiter key. Reusing one spoofed `x-real-ip` value triggers the limit after five failures, but rotating spoofed values yields a fresh bucket each time and keeps the endpoint in the normal invalid-password path. A local verification patch that bound the limiter to Astro's server-provided `clientAddress` caused the sixth rotated request to return the rate-limit response, confirming the root cause and remediation.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Derive the dashboard login rate-limit key from a server-controlled client address, such as Astro's `clientAddress`, instead of directly trusting `x-real-ip` or `x-forwarded-for` from the request.\n2. Keep proxy-header parsing behind an explicit trusted-proxy boundary if those headers must be used elsewhere.\n3. Re-test the login flow to ensure that repeated failed attempts from the same client are throttled even when proxy headers are rotated.\n4. Review other uses of the shared rate-limit helper and update them to use the same server-controlled client identity source where appropriate.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send six same-origin `POST` requests to `http://127.0.0.1:4321/dashboard/login` with an invalid password and the same spoofed `x-real-ip` header value.\n2. Observe that attempts `1` through `5` redirect to `/dashboard?error=1`, while attempt `6` redirects to `/dashboard?error=2`.\n3. Repeat the test with a fresh series of invalid login attempts, but change the spoofed `x-real-ip` value on every request.\n4. Observe that each rotated request redirects to `/dashboard?error=1` instead of reaching `/dashboard?error=2`.\n5. Apply the fix that uses Astro's server-side `clientAddress` for the rate-limit key.\n6. Repeat the rotated-header sequence and observe that the sixth request is again redirected to `/dashboard?error=2`, showing that header rotation no longer bypasses the throttle.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
